# Binary Search

## Problem Summary

Given a sorted array and a target value V, find the zero-based index of V in the array.

## Approach

Binary search is used because the input array is already sorted.

1. Set left to the first index.
2. Set right to the last index.
3. Find the middle index.
4. If the middle element equals the target, return its index.
5. If the middle element is smaller than the target, search the right half.
6. Otherwise, search the left half.
7. Continue until the target is found.

## Algorithm

The search space is divided into half during every iteration.

## Time Complexity

O(log N)

## Auxiliary Space Complexity

O(1)

## Why This Approach Is Efficient

Binary search eliminates half of the remaining search space after every comparison. Therefore, it is much faster than linear search for large sorted arrays.

## Alternative Approach

Linear search can check every element from left to right. Its time complexity is O(N), which is less efficient than binary search.

